Certainly! Below is an example of how you might implement a simple raycasting algorithm in GLSL, specifically using the WebGL 2 API which supports both vertex and fragment shaders.

### Vertex Shader

The vertex shader will pass through the position of each vertex without any transformation since we're not dealing with complex geometry transformations here.

```glsl
#version 300 es
in vec4 a_position; // Input attribute from JavaScript

void main() {
    gl_Position = a_position;
}
```

### Fragment Shader

The fragment shader will perform the actual raycasting calculations. This example assumes that the scene consists of walls at specific positions and colors based on their distance from the camera.

```glsl
#version 300 es
precision mediump float;

uniform vec2 u_resolution; // Resolution of the canvas
uniform vec2 u_cameraPosition; // Position of the camera
uniform sampler2D u_texture; // Texture to sample if needed

out vec4 fragColor; // Output color

// Function to calculate the intersection point between a ray and a wall
vec2 intersect(vec2 origin, vec2 direction, vec2 wallStart, vec2 wallEnd) {
    float t = dot(wallStart - origin, wallEnd - wallStart) / dot(direction, wallEnd - wallStart);
    return origin + t * direction;
}

void main() {
    vec2 uv = gl_FragCoord.xy / u_resolution; // Normalized coordinates
    vec2 rayDirection = normalize(vec2(uv.x * 2.0 - 1.0, -(uv.y * 2.0 - 1.0))); // Calculate ray direction

    // Define some walls for simplicity
    vec2 wallPositions[4] = vec2[](vec2(0.5, 0.0), vec2(0.5, 1.0), vec2(0.0, 0.5), vec2(1.0, 0.5));

    float minDistance = 1000.0; // Initialize minimum distance to a large value
    vec2 closestIntersection = vec2(-1.0); // Initialize closest intersection point

    for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
        vec2 intersection = intersect(u_cameraPosition, rayDirection, wallPositions[i], wallPositions[(i + 1) % 4]);

        float distanceToWall = length(intersection - u_cameraPosition);

        if (distanceToWall < minDistance && distanceToWall > 0.0) {
            minDistance = distanceToWall;
            closestIntersection = intersection;
        }
    }

    // If no intersection found, set color to black
    if (closestIntersection == vec2(-1.0)) {
        fragColor = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    } else {
        // Otherwise, use the texture or another method to determine the color
        fragColor = texture(u_texture, closestIntersection / u_resolution);
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic framework for raycasting in GLSL. You would need to adjust it according to your specific requirements, such as adding more sophisticated lighting models, handling multiple objects, or implementing different textures. Additionally, ensure that the `u_texture` uniform is properly set up in your JavaScript code when rendering this shader program.

