Here is a Java program that prompts the user to enter two positive integers and displays their greatest common divisor (GCD):

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GCD {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter first positive integer: ");
        int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter second positive integer: ");
        int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

        int gcd = findGCD(num1, num2);
        System.out.println("The GCD of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is: " + gcd);

        scanner.close();
    }

    private static int findGCD(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

This program uses the Euclidean algorithm to calculate the GCD of two numbers. The `findGCD` method takes two integers as input and returns their GCD. The main method prompts the user to enter two positive integers, calls the `findGCD` method with these inputs, and then prints the result.

To run this program, you can copy and paste it into a file named `GCD.java`, compile it using `javac GCD.java`, and then execute it using `java GCD`. When prompted, enter two positive integers separated by a space or newline character, and the program will display their GCD.

