```matlab
% Water Tank Dimensions (m)
L = 0.42; % Length
W = 0.36; % Width

% Wind Speed (m/s)
U_wind = 2.5;

% Grid Resolution
dx = 0.01;
dy = dx;
nx = floor(L / dx) + 1;
ny = floor(W / dy) + 1;

% Time Parameters
dt = 0.01; % Time step (s)
T_total = 10; % Total time for simulation (s)
nt = ceil(T_total / dt);

% Initialize Variables
x = linspace(0, L, nx);
y = linspace(0, W, ny);
[X, Y] = meshgrid(x, y);
h = zeros(ny, nx); % Surface height
u = zeros(ny, nx); % Horizontal velocity component
v = zeros(ny, nx); % Vertical velocity component

% Wave Properties
g = 9.81; % Acceleration due to gravity (m/s^2)
k = 2 * pi / lambda; % Wavenumber (assuming wavelength lambda)

% Simulation Loop
for t = 1:nt
    % Update surface height using shallow water equations
    h_new = h + dt * (-u .* diff(h, 1, 2) - v .* diff(h, 2, 1)) ./ dx;

    % Apply boundary conditions
    h_new(:, end) = h_new(:, end-1); % No-slip at right edge
    h_new(:, 1) = h_new(:, 2);      % No-slip at left edge

    % Update velocities using shallow water equations
    u_new = u + dt * (-u .* diff(u, 1, 2) - v .* diff(u, 2, 1) + g * diff(h, 1, 2)) ./ dx;
    v_new = v + dt * (-u .* diff(v, 1, 2) - v .* diff(v, 2, 1) + g * diff(h, 2, 1)) ./ dy;

    % Apply boundary conditions
    u_new(end, :) = u_new(end-1, :); % No-slip at bottom
    u_new(1, :) = u_new(2, :);       % No-slip at top
    v_new(end, :) = v_new(end-1, :); % No-slip at bottom
    v_new(1, :) = v_new(2, :);       % No-slip at top

    % Add wind forcing term
    u_new(:, end) = u_new(:, end) + U_wind * dt;

    % Update variables for next iteration
    h = h_new;
    u = u_new;
    v = v_new;

    % Plotting
    figure;
    surf(X, Y, h');
    title(['Time = ', num2str(t*dt), ' s']);
    xlabel('Length (m)');
    ylabel('Width (m)');
    zlabel('Surface Height (m)');
    colorbar;
    drawnow;
end
```

This script sets up a simple grid representing the water tank and uses finite difference methods to solve the shallow water equations with added wind forcing terms. It then plots the surface height of the water over time. Note that this is a simplified model and may not capture all complexities of real-world scenarios but should give you a good starting point for understanding the behavior of water waves under these conditions.

