To send a POST request with a client certificate in C#, you can use the `HttpClient` class along with the `SslClientAuthenticationOptions` class to specify the client certificate. Below is an example of how to do this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Path to your client certificate file (e.g., .pfx or .cer)
        string certPath = "path/to/your/certificate.pfx";

        // Password for the certificate if it's encrypted
        string certPassword = "your_certificate_password";

        // Create a new HttpClient instance
        using (var httpClient = new HttpClient())
        {
            // Load the client certificate from the specified path and password
            X509Certificate2 clientCert = new X509Certificate2(certPath, certPassword);

            // Configure SSL/TLS options to include the client certificate
            var sslOptions = new SslClientAuthenticationOptions
            {
                ClientCertificates = { clientCert }
            };

            // Set the handler for the HttpClient to use the configured SSL options
            httpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Accept.Clear();
            httpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Accept.Add(new System.Net.Http.Headers.MediaTypeWithQualityHeaderValue("application/json"));

            // Define the URL to which the POST request will be sent
            string url = "https://example.com/api/resource";

            // Prepare the content to be sent in the POST request
            var content = new StringContent("{\"key\":\"value\"}", System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

            try
            {
                // Send the POST request asynchronously
                HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.PostAsync(url, content).ConfigureAwait(false);

                // Ensure that the request was successful
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

                // Read the response content as a string
                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);

                Console.WriteLine($"Response: {responseBody}");
            }
            catch (HttpRequestException e)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Request error: {e.Message}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **Loading the Certificate**: The `X509Certificate2` object loads the client certificate from the specified path and password.
2. **Configuring SSL Options**: The `SslClientAuthenticationOptions` class is used to configure the SSL/TLS settings, including adding the client certificate.
3. **Setting Up HttpClient**: The `HttpClient` instance is set up to use the configured SSL options.
4. **Sending the Request**: A POST request is sent to the specified URL with the provided JSON content.
5. **Handling the Response**: The response is read and printed to the console.

Make sure to replace `"path/to/your/certificate.pfx"` and `"your_certificate_password"` with the actual path to your certificate file and its password, respectively. Also, adjust the URL and content according to your specific requirements.

To extract hashtags from a given text in C#, you can use regular expressions (regex). Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "This is a sample text with #hashtags and @mentions like #CSharp and #Regex.";

        // Define the regex pattern for matching hashtags
        string hashtagPattern = @"#\w+";

        // Use Regex.Matches to find all matches in the input text
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(inputText, hashtagPattern);

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:
- The `inputText` variable contains the text from which we want to extract hashtags.
- The `hashtagPattern` defines the regex pattern that matches any word starting with a hash symbol (`#`) followed by one or more word characters (`\w+`).
- The `Regex.Matches` method finds all occurrences of the pattern in the input text.
- Finally, the program prints out each extracted hashtag.

When you run this program, it will output:
```
Extracted Hashtags:
#hashtags
#CSharp
#Regex
```

This approach ensures that only valid hashtags are extracted from the text.

